{'group': 'remember-understand', 'level': 'understand', 'marks': 12, 'chapters': [1, 2],
 'q': 'Explain how the Fourier transform is obtained from the Fourier series by letting the period tend to infinity, state the conditions for its existence, and interpret the resulting X(ω).',
 'outline': ['Start from cₙ of a periodic extension x<sub>T₀</sub>(t)', 'Define X(ω) as the envelope T₀cₙ = X(nω₀)', 'Let T₀ → ∞: ω₀ → dω, the sum becomes an integral',
             'Write the forward and inverse transforms', 'Dirichlet conditions; transforms with impulses for power signals', 'X(ω) is a density, check with a pulse'],
 'answer': [
  'Take an aperiodic pulse x(t) that is zero outside |t| < T/2. Build its periodic extension x<sub>T₀</sub>(t) by repeating it every T₀ > T seconds. Inside one period the two signals are the same.',
  'The periodic signal has a Fourier series with ω₀ = 2π/T₀ and cₙ = (1/T₀)∫<sub>−T₀/2</sub><sup>T₀/2</sup>x(t)e<sup>−jnω₀t</sup>dt. Because x(t) is zero outside |t| < T/2, the limits can be stretched to ±∞.',
  'Define the envelope X(ω) = ∫<sub>−∞</sub><sup>∞</sup>x(t)e<sup>−jωt</sup>dt. Then T₀cₙ = X(nω₀): the scaled coefficients are samples of one fixed function, taken every ω₀ rad/s.',
  'Rewrite the synthesis equation with 1/T₀ = ω₀/2π: x<sub>T₀</sub>(t) = Σ cₙe<sup>jnω₀t</sup> = (1/2π)Σ X(nω₀)e<sup>jnω₀t</sup>ω₀.',
  'Now let T₀ → ∞. The copies move away to infinity, so x<sub>T₀</sub>(t) → x(t). The spacing ω₀ shrinks to dω, nω₀ becomes a continuous variable ω, and the sum becomes an integral: <b>x(t) = (1/2π)∫<sub>−∞</sub><sup>∞</sup>X(ω)e<sup>jωt</sup>dω</b>, with <b>X(ω) = ∫<sub>−∞</sub><sup>∞</sup>x(t)e<sup>−jωt</sup>dt</b>.',
  'Existence (Dirichlet conditions, sufficient): x(t) absolutely integrable, ∫|x(t)|dt < ∞, with finitely many maxima, minima and finite jumps in any finite interval. Then X(ω) exists and is continuous; at a jump the inverse transform returns the midpoint. Power signals such as 1, u(t), sgn(t) and cos ω₀t fail the first condition but get transforms with impulses: 2πδ(ω), πδ(ω) + 1/(jω), 2/(jω), π[δ(ω−ω₀) + δ(ω+ω₀)].',
  'Interpretation. The Fourier series has lines with finite heights cₙ. The transform has no lines: X(ω) is a spectral density in (units of x)·s, that is, amplitude per unit of frequency. |X(ω)| says how strongly frequency ω is present relative to its neighbours, ∠X(ω) gives its phase, and (1/2π)|X(ω)|²dω is the energy in the band dω.',
  'Two facts follow at once from the definition. X(0) = ∫x(t)dt is the area under the signal; for x(t) = e<sup>−2t</sup>u(t), X(ω) = 1/(2 + jω) and X(0) = 0.5 V·s, the area of the exponential. And for real x(t), X(−ω) = X*(ω), so only ω ≥ 0 needs to be plotted: |X| is even and ∠X is odd.',
  {'text': 'Check with a pulse of height 1 and width 2 s (|t| < 1). Its transform is X(ω) = 2 sin ω/ω = 2Sa(ω). Its periodic extension with T₀ = 4 s has cₙ = sin(nπ/2)/(nπ), so T₀cₙ should equal X(nω₀):',
   'table': T(['T₀', 'ω₀ (rad/s)', 'T₀c₁', 'X(ω₀) = 2 sin ω₀/ω₀'], [['4 s', 'π/2 = 1.571', '4/π = 1.273', '1.273'], ['8 s', 'π/4 = 0.785', '1.801', '1.801']])},
  'Practical reading: a signal that lasts longer in time has a narrower X(ω), and a short one a wider X(ω). The pulse of width 2 s has its first spectral zero at ω = π rad/s; a pulse of width 1 s would have it at 2π rad/s.',
  'Closing check: doubling the period halves the line spacing (1.571 → 0.785 rad/s) while every scaled line still sits on the same curve 2 sin ω/ω. In the limit the lines fill the curve, which is the Fourier transform.',
 ]},

{'group': 'remember-understand', 'level': 'remember', 'marks': 13, 'chapters': [3],
 'q': 'State and prove the following properties of the Fourier transform: linearity, time shifting, frequency shifting, time scaling, time differentiation, and convolution in time.',
 'outline': ['Write the defining integral once', 'Each property: statement, then a two-line proof by substitution', 'Name the condition (a ≠ 0, differentiable, transforms exist)', 'Verify two properties on e<sup>−2t</sup>u(t) with numbers'],
 'answer': [
  'Definition used throughout: X(ω) = ∫<sub>−∞</sub><sup>∞</sup>x(t)e<sup>−jωt</sup>dt, written x(t) ↔ X(ω); ω is in rad/s.',
  '<b>Linearity:</b> a x₁(t) + b x₂(t) ↔ aX₁(ω) + bX₂(ω) for constants a, b. Proof: the integral of a sum is the sum of the integrals, and constants come out of the integral.',
  '<b>Time shifting:</b> x(t − t₀) ↔ e<sup>−jωt₀</sup>X(ω). Proof: ∫x(t − t₀)e<sup>−jωt</sup>dt; put u = t − t₀, dt = du: ∫x(u)e<sup>−jω(u + t₀)</sup>du = e<sup>−jωt₀</sup>X(ω). A delay keeps |X| and adds a phase −ωt₀ that grows linearly with ω.',
  '<b>Frequency shifting:</b> e<sup>jω₀t</sup>x(t) ↔ X(ω − ω₀). Proof: ∫x(t)e<sup>jω₀t</sup>e<sup>−jωt</sup>dt = ∫x(t)e<sup>−j(ω−ω₀)t</sup>dt = X(ω − ω₀). With cos ω₀t = (e<sup>jω₀t</sup> + e<sup>−jω₀t</sup>)/2 this gives the modulation theorem x(t)cos ω₀t ↔ ½[X(ω−ω₀) + X(ω+ω₀)].',
  '<b>Time scaling:</b> x(at) ↔ (1/|a|)X(ω/a), a real and nonzero. Proof for a > 0: put u = at, dt = du/a: ∫x(u)e<sup>−j(ω/a)u</sup>du/a = (1/a)X(ω/a). For a < 0 the limits swap, which gives the factor 1/|a|. Squeezing in time (a > 1) stretches the spectrum and lowers it.',
  '<b>Time differentiation:</b> dx/dt ↔ jωX(ω), if x(t) → 0 as |t| → ∞. Proof: differentiate the inverse transform, x(t) = (1/2π)∫X(ω)e<sup>jωt</sup>dω, under the integral sign: dx/dt = (1/2π)∫[jωX(ω)]e<sup>jωt</sup>dω, so dx/dt is the inverse transform of jωX(ω).',
  '<b>Convolution in time:</b> x(t)*h(t) ↔ X(ω)H(ω). Proof: Y(ω) = ∫[∫x(τ)h(t − τ)dτ]e<sup>−jωt</sup>dt. Swap the order and put u = t − τ: Y(ω) = ∫x(τ)e<sup>−jωτ</sup>[∫h(u)e<sup>−jωu</sup>du]dτ = H(ω)∫x(τ)e<sup>−jωτ</sup>dτ = X(ω)H(ω).',
  'Why the convolution property matters: the output of an LTI system is y = x * h, so Y(ω) = X(ω)H(ω), and a convolution integral becomes a product. Example: e<sup>−t</sup>u(t) * e<sup>−2t</sup>u(t) ↔ 1/[(1 + jω)(2 + jω)] = 1/(1 + jω) − 1/(2 + jω), so the convolution is (e<sup>−t</sup> − e<sup>−2t</sup>)u(t); at ω = 0 both sides give ½, the area of the result (1 − ½).',
  {'text': 'Numerical check on x(t) = e<sup>−2t</sup>u(t), X(ω) = 1/(2 + jω), at ω = 2 rad/s (so |X(2)| = 1/√8 = 0.3536):',
   'table': T(['Property', 'New signal', 'Direct transform', 'Property says', 'Value at ω = 2'],
              [['Time shift, t₀ = 1 s', 'e<sup>−2(t−1)</sup>u(t−1)', 'e<sup>−jω</sup>/(2+jω)', 'e<sup>−jω</sup>X(ω)', '|·| = 0.3536, phase −45° − 114.6°'],
               ['Scaling, a = 2', 'e<sup>−4t</sup>u(t)', '1/(4+jω)', '½X(ω/2) = ½/(2+jω/2) = 1/(4+jω)', '1/√20 = 0.2236'],
               ['Differentiation', 'δ(t) − 2e<sup>−2t</sup>u(t)', '1 − 2/(2+jω) = jω/(2+jω)', 'jωX(ω)', '2/√8 = 0.7071']])},
  'Closing check: scaling by a = 2 gives 0.2236 both ways, and the derivative row matches exactly, since d/dt[e<sup>−2t</sup>u(t)] = δ(t) − 2e<sup>−2t</sup>u(t) (the jump of height 1 at t = 0 gives the impulse). A shift of 1 s adds −2 rad = −114.6° at ω = 2 and leaves the magnitude 0.3536 unchanged.',
 ]},

{'group': 'remember-understand', 'level': 'understand', 'marks': 13, 'chapters': [1, 4],
 'q': "State and prove Parseval's theorem for a periodic signal, give the energy form for an aperiodic signal, and define the energy and power spectral densities.",
 'outline': ['Periodic: P = (1/T₀)∫|x|²dt = Σ|cₙ|²', 'Proof: substitute the series for x*(t), use orthogonality', 'Aperiodic: E = (1/2π)∫|X(ω)|²dω, proof via the inverse transform',
             'ESD |X(ω)|² in J per rad/s; PSD as a limit, in W per rad/s', 'Check both forms with numbers'],
 'answer': [
  'For a periodic signal of period T₀ with exponential Fourier coefficients cₙ: <b>P = (1/T₀)∫<sub>T₀</sub>|x(t)|²dt = Σ<sub>n=−∞</sub><sup>∞</sup>|cₙ|²</b>. P is the average power in watts across 1 Ω.',
  'Proof. |x(t)|² = x(t)x*(t), and x*(t) = Σ cₙ* e<sup>−jnω₀t</sup>. So P = (1/T₀)∫<sub>T₀</sub>x(t)Σcₙ*e<sup>−jnω₀t</sup>dt = Σcₙ*[(1/T₀)∫<sub>T₀</sub>x(t)e<sup>−jnω₀t</sup>dt] = Σcₙ*cₙ = Σ|cₙ|². The bracket is exactly the analysis integral for cₙ; orthogonality is what made that integral pick out one coefficient.',
  'Meaning: each harmonic carries power |cₙ|² on each side, so a real cosine of amplitude Aₙ carries 2|cₙ|² = Aₙ²/2, the familiar mean square of a sinusoid. The powers add because the harmonics are orthogonal: cross terms average to zero.',
  'For an aperiodic energy signal: <b>E = ∫<sub>−∞</sub><sup>∞</sup>|x(t)|²dt = (1/2π)∫<sub>−∞</sub><sup>∞</sup>|X(ω)|²dω</b>. Proof: E = ∫x(t)x*(t)dt, and x*(t) = (1/2π)∫X*(ω)e<sup>−jωt</sup>dω. Swap the integrals: E = (1/2π)∫X*(ω)[∫x(t)e<sup>−jωt</sup>dt]dω = (1/2π)∫X*(ω)X(ω)dω = (1/2π)∫|X(ω)|²dω.',
  'Energy spectral density: Ψ(ω) = |X(ω)|², in J per rad/s. The energy in a band ω₁ ≤ |ω| ≤ ω₂ is (1/2π) times its area over both the positive and negative band.',
  'Power spectral density: for a power signal, truncate it to x<sub>T</sub>(t) (zero outside |t| < T/2) with transform X<sub>T</sub>(ω), and define S(ω) = lim<sub>T→∞</sub>|X<sub>T</sub>(ω)|²/T, in W per rad/s. Then P = (1/2π)∫S(ω)dω. For a periodic signal S(ω) = 2πΣ|cₙ|²δ(ω − nω₀), and the integral gives back Σ|cₙ|².',
  'In trigonometric form the same theorem reads P = (a₀/2)² + Σ<sub>n≥1</sub>(aₙ² + bₙ²)/2, because |cₙ|² + |c<sub>−n</sub>|² = 2 × ¼(aₙ² + bₙ²) = (aₙ² + bₙ²)/2: the DC power plus half the squared amplitude of each harmonic.',
  'Units: E in joules and P in watts for a voltage across 1 Ω; Ψ(ω) in J per rad/s and S(ω) in W per rad/s. If frequency is in hertz the 1/2π disappears: E = ∫|X(f)|²df.',
  {'text': 'Periodic check: the pulse train with height 1, |t| < 1 s high, T₀ = 4 s has c₀ = 0.5 and cₙ = sin(nπ/2)/(nπ).',
   'table': T(['Term', 'Power', 'Running total', 'Share of 0.5 W'],
              [['n = 0', '0.25', '0.25', '50%'], ['n = ±1', '2/π² = 0.2026', '0.4526', '90.5%'], ['n = ±3', '2/(9π²) = 0.0225', '0.4752', '95.0%'], ['n = ±5', '2/(25π²) = 0.0081', '0.4833', '96.7%'], ['all odd n', '(2/π²)(π²/8) = 0.25 in total', '0.5', '100%']])},
  'The time side gives P = (1/4)∫<sub>−1</sub><sup>1</sup>1 dt = 0.5 W, the same as the full sum.',
  'Use of the ESD, the energy bandwidth: for x(t) = e<sup>−3t</sup>u(t) the energy in |ω| ≤ B is a fraction (2/π)tan<sup>−1</sup>(B/3) of the total. Setting this to 0.9 gives tan<sup>−1</sup>(B/3) = 81°, so B = 3 tan 81° = 18.94 rad/s holds 90% of the energy.',
  'Aperiodic check: x(t) = e<sup>−3t</sup>u(t). Time side: ∫<sub>0</sub><sup>∞</sup>e<sup>−6t</sup>dt = 1/6 J. Frequency side: |X(ω)|² = 1/(9 + ω²), and (1/2π)∫dω/(9 + ω²) = (1/2π)(π/3) = 1/6 J. Both give 0.1667 J, which closes the proof with numbers.',
 ]},

